        ":question/math-academy-id": "q-73457",
        ":question/problem": "Given that $a=⟨1,-3,2⟩,$ $b=⟨2,-1,-1⟩,$ and $c=⟨3,-3,0⟩,$ find $a\\cdot (b\\times c)$ using the definition of the scalar triple product.",
        ":question/worked-solution": "First, we find the cross product:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}b\\times c & =|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ 2 & -1 & -1 \\\\ 3 & -3 & 0\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}-1 & -1 \\\\ -3 & 0\\end{aligned}|i-|\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 \\\\ 3 & 0\\end{aligned}|j+|\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 \\\\ 3 & -3\\end{aligned}|k \\\\  & =((-1)\\cdot 0-(-1)\\cdot (-3))i-(2\\cdot 0-(-1)\\cdot 3)j+(2\\cdot (-3)-(-1)\\cdot 3)k \\\\  & =-3i-3j-3k \\\\  & =⟨-3,-3,-3⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can compute the scalar triple product:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a\\cdot (b\\times c) & =⟨1,-3,2⟩\\cdot ⟨-3,-3,-3⟩ \\\\  & =1\\cdot (-3)+(-3)\\cdot (-3)+2\\cdot (-3) \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$"
      },
